-- name    : ErschlerZheng.evalFree_zetaFree_congr_and_zetaHat_evalFree_eq_of_tail_ne
-- status  : Proved
-- author  : @dbenbenn
-- created : 2026-10-06T00:57:19.488562+00:00
-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/51e4b918-c217-4cda-bcf8-be15424445b3
-- title:
--   p. 15 — unless ω's tail after n is constant at a letter ≠ ω_n, ζ_{ω_n} is a well-defined homomorphism on ⟨ab, ac, ad⟩; with this at every level ⩽ n, so is ζ_{ω_0}∘⋯∘ζ_{ω_n}
-- statement:
--   Let $\omega \in \{\mathbf 0, \mathbf 1, \mathbf 2\}^{\mathbb N}$ and $n \in \mathbb N$, and assume that for every letter $i \neq \omega_n$ some $k > n$ has $\omega_k \neq i$: the tail $\omega_{n+1}\omega_{n+2}\ldots$ is not the constant string of a letter other than $\omega_n$. For $k \in \mathbb N$ let $e_k$ (`evalFree ω k`) evaluate words in $ab, ac, ad$ and their inverses in $G_{\mathfrak s^k\omega}$, with $b, c, d$ read as $b_{\mathfrak s^k\omega}, c_{\mathfrak s^k\omega}, d_{\mathfrak s^k\omega}$, and let $\hat\zeta_k$ (`zetaHat ω k`) be $\zeta_{\omega_k}$ on group elements, computed on a chosen word. Then:
--
--   1. if $e_{n+1}(w_1) = e_{n+1}(w_2)$, then $e_n(\zeta_{\omega_n}(w_1)) = e_n(\zeta_{\omega_n}(w_2))$;
--   2. $\hat\zeta_n(e_{n+1}(w)) = e_n(\zeta_{\omega_n}(w))$ for every word $w$;
--   3. $\hat\zeta_n(gh) = \hat\zeta_n(g)\,\hat\zeta_n(h)$ for $g, h$ in the image of $e_{n+1}$, the subgroup of $G_{\mathfrak s^{n+1}\omega}$ generated by $ab, ac, ad$;
--   4. for every $k \le n$, if the same condition on the tail holds at every level $j$ with $k \le j \le n$ (for every letter $i \neq \omega_j$ some $m > j$ has $\omega_m \neq i$), then $\hat\zeta_k \circ \hat\zeta_{k+1} \circ \cdots \circ \hat\zeta_n$ maps $e_{n+1}(w)$ to $e_k(\zeta_{\omega_k} \circ \cdots \circ \zeta_{\omega_n}(w))$, the value in $G_{\mathfrak s^k\omega}$ of the substituted word, for every word $w$; at $k = 0$ the value is in $G_\omega$.
--
--   Erschler and Zheng, p. 15: “It follows from (2.3) that if $g \in G_{\mathfrak s^{n+1}\omega}$ can be represented by a word in $\{ab_{\mathfrak s^{n+1}\omega}, ac_{\mathfrak s^{n+1}\omega}, ad_{\mathfrak s^{n+1}\omega}\}$, then the substitution $\zeta_{\omega_n}$ can be applied to $g$ and the resulting image in $G_{\mathfrak s^n\omega}$ does not depend on the choice of the representing word. The composition $\zeta_{\omega_0} \circ \ldots \circ \zeta_{\omega_n}$ maps such $g \in G_{\mathfrak s^{n+1}\omega}$ into $G_\omega$.”
--
--   The hypothesis on the tail is not printed; without it the claim is false ([`ErschlerZheng.not_forall_evalWord_zetaWord_congr`](https://prove2.me/theorems/6eacbdd8-4d7b-4e14-b1db-e26bb207c258)). Words may use the inverses $ba, ca, da$ as well, so “represented by a word” covers the whole subgroup generated by $ab, ac, ad$, and conjunct 1 restricted to positive words is the printed claim. Conjunct 3 says that $\hat\zeta_n$ is a homomorphism on that subgroup. Conjunct 4 is the last sentence of the quotation, at every stage of the composition: each partial composite $\hat\zeta_k \circ \cdots \circ \hat\zeta_n$, applied to an element, is computed on any representing word, and its value is the value of a word at level $k$, where $\hat\zeta_{k-1}$ applies; at $k = 0$ the composition lands in $G_\omega$. So the composition does not depend on the words chosen at the levels below $n$. It assumes the condition on the tail at the levels $k, \ldots, n$; whether the composition is already well defined under the condition at level $n$ alone is not decided here.
-- source:
--   Erschler, A. and Zheng, T., Growth of periodic Grigorchuk groups, Invent. Math. 219 (2020) 1069–1155, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-019-00922-0 (arXiv:1802.09077v2, whose page numbers are used), p. 15, the substitutions act on group elements

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ErschlerZheng_Grigorchuk

namespace ErschlerZheng

theorem evalFree_zetaFree_congr_and_zetaHat_evalFree_eq_of_tail_ne (ω : ℕ → Fin 3) (n : ℕ)
    (hω : ∀ i : Fin 3, i ≠ ω n → ∃ k, n < k ∧ ω k ≠ i) :
    (∀ w₁ w₂ : FreeGroup APair, evalFree ω (n + 1) w₁ = evalFree ω (n + 1) w₂ →
      evalFree ω n (zetaFree (ω n) w₁) = evalFree ω n (zetaFree (ω n) w₂)) ∧
    (∀ w : FreeGroup APair, zetaHat ω n (evalFree ω (n + 1) w) = evalFree ω n (zetaFree (ω n) w)) ∧
    (∀ g ∈ (evalFree ω (n + 1)).range, ∀ h ∈ (evalFree ω (n + 1)).range,
      zetaHat ω n (g * h) = zetaHat ω n g * zetaHat ω n h) ∧
    ∀ k ≤ n, (∀ j, k ≤ j → j ≤ n → ∀ i : Fin 3, i ≠ ω j → ∃ m, j < m ∧ ω m ≠ i) →
      ∀ w : FreeGroup APair,
        (List.Ico k (n + 1)).foldr (fun j g => zetaHat ω j g) (evalFree ω (n + 1) w) =
          evalFree ω k ((List.Ico k (n + 1)).foldr (fun j w => zetaFree (ω j) w) w) := by
  sorry

end ErschlerZheng
